; How much damage a 制限装甲 enemy swallowed, said in the battle log:
;
;   Rance's Rance Attack！ resulted in…Limit Broken！ (7340 raw)
;   Stone Guardian took 100 of damage！
;
;   Rance's Rance Attack！ resulted in…Cannot deal damage！(Limit Break) (1780 absorbed)
;
; Half of a feature a build can leave out; the other half is the switch,
; absorbed_damage_in_log.jaf beside it, and feature.js in this folder names the
; pair. docs/absorbed-damage-log.md is the write-up.
;
; WHAT THE GAME DOES. 制限装甲 is skill effect 107, and its value is the
; threshold N -- twelve tiers, 5 000 to 2 000 000, in
; archives/Rance10EX_v1_04/11_スキルデータ.x. PlayerAttackDamageCalculator@CalcSpecificAegisEffect
; (FUNC 26978) compares the finished damage against it:
;
;     if (damage > N)  GuardType = GuardBreak;                        -> exactly 100 lands
;     else           { GuardType = Guard; GuardReason = DamageRange; } -> nothing lands
;
; Either way the number the player rolled is dropped. DamageInformation@TotalDamage
; (FUNC 27053) answers 0 or 100 from then on, and only @PreGuardDamage (FUNC 27055)
; -- the base damage plus every additional damage, which is the figure that was
; compared against N -- still knows it. Nothing prints it. On an absorbed hit it
; is drawn over the enemy and counted down to zero (DamageView@MotionNumber,
; FUNC 25071, takes the same @PreGuardDamage), so a player who looks away has no
; way back to it; on a break it is not drawn at all, because the flying number
; there is the flat 100.
;
; WHY THIS FUNCTION. SceneBattle@ShowLogGuard is the one place the guard is put
; into words, it has the DamageInformation as an argument, and it has one caller
; -- SceneBattle@ShowAttackDamage (FUNC 24913), the OnDamageEvent handler, which
; reaches it only for a player attack. So both guard outcomes pass through here
; and nothing else does.
;
; It also already holds three ref BattleLog locals, which is the one thing a .jam
; cannot arrange: --jam rewrites code and leaves the FUNC section alone, so a
; patched function has exactly the locals it was compiled with, and building a
; log needs one. The tail below reuses `log` and the `new BattleLog` dummy the
; third branch already uses, after that branch has finished with them.
;
; WHY THE TWO ENGLISH LITERALS. A literal in a .jam is matched against the
; string table by *text*, and the text patch has already run by the time
; alice-tools reads this file. Pushing the Japanese s[3295] / s[3296] here would
; match no slot -- they say English by that point -- and come back as two new
; Japanese strings that this function would push, which is the fault
; patches/leader_state_names.jam exists for. So the English is written in
; directly.
;
; Which slot it then lands on is not something to rely on, and measuring it says
; so: applied on its own over the rendered dialogue the two literals resolve to
; s[3295] and s[3296] themselves, and in a real build -- the same text patch,
; with the English .jaf and .jam ahead of this one -- they come back as two new
; slots holding the same sentences. Both are correct, because a duplicate
; holding identical text is what the player reads either way, and both leave
; s[3295] / s[3296] alone for the callers that keep them (the unpatched function
; a --without= build runs, and SkillEffectProcessDamageSelf@GetLog, which pushes
; s[3296] too).
;
; What that does mean is that the wording here is load-bearing rather than
; decorative: with the feature on it is what the player reads, with it off the
; cherry-picked slot is, and nothing in a diff would show the two drifting
; apart. So modules/GuardLog.js holds these two lines against what
; patches/system_cherry_picks.v1.04.ain.txt assigns to those slots at the start
; of every build: reword one and the build stops until the other says the same
; thing.
;
; The cost is the one thing this feature is not free of: a --text-lang=jp build
; with it in shows those two lines in English. It builds and it plays, which is
; what that build is for, but it is the reason to leave the third line -- the
; number itself -- language-neutral. " raw" and " absorbed" are the only words
; this feature invents.
;
; The body is FUNC 24930 verbatim from
;
;   alice ain dump -c game/ain/Rance10.v1.04.ain
;
; with the two strings swapped, one JUMP retargeted and the block at the bottom
; added. Regenerate it the same way if the game version changes: every number
; here is an index into one build of one .ain.
;
; Save this file as UTF-8 with no BOM. The assembler reads a byte order mark as
; an instruction and stops on line 1 -- "Invalid instruction: ϻ" -- and an editor
; that defaults .jam to UTF-8-with-BOM will quietly put one back.
FUNC 24930
	.LOCALREF info
	PUSH 2
	REF
	PUSH 2
	EQUALE
	IFNZ 0x3c27bc
	JUMP 0x3c2828
; GuardType == GuardBreak: "Limit Broken！", and the 100 that lands is logged
; after this by SceneBattle@ShowAdditionalDamageLog. The only edit is the JUMP,
; which used to go straight to the RETURN.
0x3c27bc:
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 24944
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 24931
	CALLMETHOD 0
	.STACK_LOCALASSIGN "<dummy : 右辺値参照化用>"
	CALLMETHOD 1
	.LOCALDELETE "<dummy : 右辺値参照化用>"
	.LOCALDELETE "<dummy : 右辺値参照化用>"
	JUMP show_raw_damage
0x3c2828:
	.LOCALREF info
	PUSH 3
	REF
	PUSH 2
	EQUALE
	IFNZ 0x3c284e
	JUMP 0x3c2868
; MajinShieldType == GuardBreak: the Fiend's barrier is gone and the damage
; landed in full, so ShowAdditionalDamageLog prints it and there is nothing here
; to say. Left exactly as it was, JUMP to the RETURN included.
0x3c284e:
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 24943
	S_PUSH "The Fiend's invincible barrier was removed！"
	CALLMETHOD 1
	JUMP 0x3c29a2
0x3c2868:
	.LOCALREF info
	PUSH 4
	REF
	PUSH 0
	NOTE
	IFNZ 0x3c288e
	JUMP 0x3c29a2
; GuardReason != None: "Cannot deal damage！(<Any>)", <Any> being
; DamageInformation@GuardReasonText (FUNC 27051) -- "Limit Break" for this one.
; Unchanged but for the string, and it now falls through to the block below
; rather than into the RETURN.
0x3c288e:
	PUSHLOCALPAGE
	PUSH 3
	DUP2
	REF
	DELETE
	.LOCALREF "<dummy : new BattleLog>"
	DELETE
	PUSHLOCALPAGE
	PUSH 4
	NEW BattleLog 27212
	ASSIGN
	ASSIGN
	SP_INC
	.LOCALDELETE "<dummy : new BattleLog>"
	.LOCALREF log
	PUSH 27225
	S_PUSH "Cannot deal damage！(<Any>)"
	DUP_X2
	A_REF
	CALLMETHOD 1
	DELETE
	.LOCALREF log
	PUSH 11
	REF
	.LOCALREF info
	PUSH 27051
	CALLMETHOD 0
	S_ASSIGN
	DELETE
	.LOCALREF log
	PUSH 9
	PUSH 1
	ASSIGN
	POP
	.LOCALREF log
	PUSH 8
	PUSH 0
	ASSIGN
	POP
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 24944
	.LOCALREF log
	CALLMETHOD 1
	.LOCALDELETE "<dummy : new BattleLog>"
	.LOCALDELETE log
; --- added: the number that was rolled, as a log entry of its own -----------
;
;   CALLFUNC AbsorbedDamageInLogEnabled   is the switch file there
;   IFZ 0x3c29a2                          no: the log reads what it always read
;   log = new BattleLog();
;   log.Text = " ({255,255,0}<Damage>{}<Any>)";
;   log.AnyString = info.GuardType == GuardBreak ? " raw" : " absorbed";
;   log.Damage = info.PreGuardDamage;     FUNC 27055 -- base + every additional
;   log.IsAddMode = true;                 append to the line, do not start one
;   log.IsShowCenterLog = false;          and no banner across the screen
;   ShowLog(log);                         FUNC 24944
;
; A log entry rather than a longer version of the two lines above, because those
; two are translation and this is the feature: the entry carries its own text, so
; turning the feature off leaves them untouched and nothing here needs a second
; English wording kept in step with anything.
;
; BattleLog@ReplaceText (FUNC 27230) fills <Damage> from the Damage member and
; <Any> from AnyString, and BattleLog@Width strips the {…} colour codes before
; measuring, so this is 16 units against BattleLogCollection@IsFlushRequired's 60
; and wraps to the next log line by itself when the sentence before it is long.
;
; IsAddMode has to be set because a BattleLog starts with IsAddMode false and
; IsShowCenterLog *true* (BattleLog@2, FUNC 27213) -- the defaults of a line that
; announces itself in the middle of the screen, which is why the number cannot
; simply go through SceneBattle@ShowLog(string).
;
; The word is chosen from GuardType rather than from which branch jumped here,
; so the two paths share one block. It matters that it is chosen at all: on a
; break the damage was not absorbed, it was replaced by a flat 100.
;
; Both branches above have finished with `log` and the `new BattleLog` dummy by
; the time they reach this label -- the third one deletes both on the line before
; -- and the GuardBreak branch never touched either, so the DELETE below runs
; against the -1 a ref local starts at. Which is what the game's own code does
; here too: the third branch deletes the same uninitialised local before its
; first assignment.
show_raw_damage:
	CALLFUNC AbsorbedDamageInLogEnabled
	IFZ 0x3c29a2
	PUSHLOCALPAGE
	PUSH 3
	DUP2
	REF
	DELETE
	.LOCALREF "<dummy : new BattleLog>"
	DELETE
	PUSHLOCALPAGE
	PUSH 4
	NEW BattleLog 27212
	ASSIGN
	ASSIGN
	SP_INC
	.LOCALDELETE "<dummy : new BattleLog>"
	.LOCALREF log
	PUSH 27225
	S_PUSH " ({255,255,0}<Damage>{}<Any>)"
	DUP_X2
	A_REF
	CALLMETHOD 1
	DELETE
	.LOCALREF log
	PUSH 11
	REF
	.LOCALREF info
	PUSH 2
	REF
	PUSH 2
	EQUALE
	IFZ absorbed_word
	S_PUSH " raw"
	JUMP raw_damage_word_done
absorbed_word:
	S_PUSH " absorbed"
raw_damage_word_done:
	S_ASSIGN
	DELETE
	.LOCALREF log
	PUSH 7
	.LOCALREF info
	PUSH 27055
	CALLMETHOD 0
	ASSIGN
	POP
	.LOCALREF log
	PUSH 9
	PUSH 1
	ASSIGN
	POP
	.LOCALREF log
	PUSH 8
	PUSH 0
	ASSIGN
	POP
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 24944
	.LOCALREF log
	CALLMETHOD 1
	.LOCALDELETE "<dummy : new BattleLog>"
	.LOCALDELETE log
; --- end of the addition ---------------------------------------------------
0x3c29a2:
	RETURN
ENDFUNC SceneBattle@ShowLogGuard
